  "id": "COMPOUND:2248 - PROTEIN:1107",
  "verbalization": "Myricetin appears to have a complex network of interactions leading to the activation of Serine/threonine-protein kinase B-raf. The evidence suggests that Myricetin targets multiple entities, including Shiga-like toxin 1 subunit B and Transcobalamin-1, which in turn are targets of 2'-O-Acetylfrangulin A. This pathway ultimately leads to the activation of B-raf kinase. Similarly, Myricetin is also shown to target Peripheral myelin protein 22, which is inhibited by Givinostat, and this inhibition also leads to B-raf activation. The final scores from the paths indicate a consistent and significant relationship between Myricetin and B-raf, suggesting a potential mechanistic link. The integration of these pathways highlights the involvement of 2'-O-Acetylfrangulin A as a central mediator in the signaling cascade initiated by Myricetin. The evidence supports a plausible mechanism where Myricetin modulates the activity of B-raf through the activation of downstream targets, though the exact nature of the interaction between Myricetin and 2'-O-Acetylfrangulin A remains to be fully elucidated.",
